{"content_hash": "55f81d4c89816352", "version_id": 292, "article_id": 30, "level_code": "en_a2", "lang": "en", "level": 2, "level_label": "A2 初级", "title": "Woody Blankets Help Save Arctic Permafrost", "title_zh": "用木头“毯子”保护北极冻土", "has_translation": true, "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03045-8", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.rawpixel.com/image_1300/cHJpdmF0ZS9sci9pbWFnZXMvd2Vic2l0ZS8yMDIyLTA0L2ZsNDg0MTYwMDkzMjYtaW1hZ2Uta3A0dndxanUuanBn.jpg", "credit": "U.S. Department of Energy · cc0 1.0 · via rawpixel"}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 249, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "A Canadian company is testing special blankets made from wood. They want to stop frozen ground from melting and releasing harmful gases.", "lead_zh": "一家加拿大公司正在测试用木头做的特殊毯子。他们想阻止冻土融化，避免有害气体释放出来。", "preview": "A Canadian start-up called PermaC is testing a new idea. They put a blanket of woody materials on frozen ground in the Arctic. The blanket h", "preview_zh": "一家名为PermaC的加拿大初创公司正在测试一个新想法。他们把一种木质材料做的毯子铺在北极的冻土上。毯子帮助地面保持寒冷和稳定。这可以阻止温室气体逃逸。Cansu Culha是PermaC的联合创始人。她说：“我们的目标是确保土壤中的碳继续埋在地下。”", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 282, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@8760f31/content/audio/30/en_a2.mp3", "duration": 103.763, "size_bytes": 626400, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 287, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@8760f31/content/data/versions/292.json", "paragraphs": ["A Canadian start-up called PermaC is testing a new idea. They put a blanket of woody materials on frozen ground in the Arctic. The blanket helps keep the ground cold and stable. This can stop greenhouse gases from escaping. Cansu Culha is a co-founder of PermaC. She says, \"Our goal is to make sure the carbon in the soil remains buried.\"", "The Arctic is getting warmer, so the frozen ground is thawing. When it thaws, the ground can collapse and erode. This creates deep holes called slumps. These slumps release carbon dioxide and methane. One estimate says fast erosion could increase permafrost emissions by 40%.", "The company just finished its first test in Canada's Northwest Territories. They covered the edge of a slump with wood shavings, soil, native seeds, and a mat made from coconut husks. During the summer, the uncovered edge moved back 5 metres. The covered area did not move. Culha says this is very promising.", "However, scientists say the result is from only one slump and one summer. It is hard to measure exactly how much CO2 the blankets prevent. Culha thinks one blanket could stop about 2,200 tonnes of CO2 a year. If they install 20,000 blankets, they might prevent 40 million tonnes a year. That is about the same as Hungary's yearly emissions.", "The company wants to sell carbon credits for the emissions they prevent. They have raised 1.4 million Canadian dollars from investors. They hope to issue credits as soon as next year."], "paragraphs_zh": ["一家名为PermaC的加拿大初创公司正在测试一个新想法。他们把一种木质材料做的毯子铺在北极的冻土上。毯子帮助地面保持寒冷和稳定。这可以阻止温室气体逃逸。Cansu Culha是PermaC的联合创始人。她说：“我们的目标是确保土壤中的碳继续埋在地下。”", "北极正在变暖，所以冻土正在融化。融化时，地面会塌陷和侵蚀。这形成了被称为“塌陷坑”的深洞。这些塌陷坑会释放二氧化碳和甲烷。一项估计表明，快速侵蚀可能使冻土排放增加40%。", "该公司刚刚在加拿大西北地区完成了第一次测试。他们用木屑、土壤、本地种子和椰子壳做的垫子覆盖了一个塌陷坑的边缘。在夏季，没有覆盖的边缘后退了5米。覆盖的区域没有移动。Culha说这非常有希望。", "然而，科学家表示，这个结果只来自一个塌陷坑和一个夏天。很难准确测量毯子阻止了多少二氧化碳。Culha认为一条毯子每年可以阻止约2200吨二氧化碳。如果他们安装2万条毯子，每年可能阻止4000万吨。这大约相当于匈牙利的年排放量。", "该公司想为他们阻止的排放出售碳信用。他们已经从投资者那里筹集了140万加元。他们希望最早明年发行信用。"], "body": "A Canadian start-up called PermaC is testing a new idea. They put a blanket of woody materials on frozen ground in the Arctic. The blanket helps keep the ground cold and stable. This can stop greenhouse gases from escaping. Cansu Culha is a co-founder of PermaC. She says, \"Our goal is to make sure the carbon in the soil remains buried.\"\n\nThe Arctic is getting warmer, so the frozen ground is thawing. When it thaws, the ground can collapse and erode. This creates deep holes called slumps. These slumps release carbon dioxide and methane. One estimate says fast erosion could increase permafrost emissions by 40%.\n\nThe company just finished its first test in Canada's Northwest Territories. They covered the edge of a slump with wood shavings, soil, native seeds, and a mat made from coconut husks. During the summer, the uncovered edge moved back 5 metres. The covered area did not move. Culha says this is very promising.\n\nHowever, scientists say the result is from only one slump and one summer. It is hard to measure exactly how much CO2 the blankets prevent. Culha thinks one blanket could stop about 2,200 tonnes of CO2 a year. If they install 20,000 blankets, they might prevent 40 million tonnes a year. That is about the same as Hungary's yearly emissions.\n\nThe company wants to sell carbon credits for the emissions they prevent. They have raised 1.4 million Canadian dollars from investors. They hope to issue credits as soon as next year.", "body_zh": "一家名为PermaC的加拿大初创公司正在测试一个新想法。他们把一种木质材料做的毯子铺在北极的冻土上。毯子帮助地面保持寒冷和稳定。这可以阻止温室气体逃逸。Cansu Culha是PermaC的联合创始人。她说：“我们的目标是确保土壤中的碳继续埋在地下。”\n\n北极正在变暖，所以冻土正在融化。融化时，地面会塌陷和侵蚀。这形成了被称为“塌陷坑”的深洞。这些塌陷坑会释放二氧化碳和甲烷。一项估计表明，快速侵蚀可能使冻土排放增加40%。\n\n该公司刚刚在加拿大西北地区完成了第一次测试。他们用木屑、土壤、本地种子和椰子壳做的垫子覆盖了一个塌陷坑的边缘。在夏季，没有覆盖的边缘后退了5米。覆盖的区域没有移动。Culha说这非常有希望。\n\n然而，科学家表示，这个结果只来自一个塌陷坑和一个夏天。很难准确测量毯子阻止了多少二氧化碳。Culha认为一条毯子每年可以阻止约2200吨二氧化碳。如果他们安装2万条毯子，每年可能阻止4000万吨。这大约相当于匈牙利的年排放量。\n\n该公司想为他们阻止的排放出售碳信用。他们已经从投资者那里筹集了140万加元。他们希望最早明年发行信用。", "text": "A Canadian start-up called PermaC is testing a new idea. They put a blanket of woody materials on frozen ground in the Arctic. The blanket helps keep the ground cold and stable. This can stop greenhouse gases from escaping. Cansu Culha is a co-founder of PermaC. She says, \"Our goal is to make sure the carbon in the soil remains buried.\" The Arctic is getting warmer, so the frozen ground is thawing. When it thaws, the ground can collapse and erode. This creates deep holes called slumps. These slumps release carbon dioxide and methane. One estimate says fast erosion could increase permafrost emissions by 40%. The company just finished its first test in Canada's Northwest Territories. They covered the edge of a slump with wood shavings, soil, native seeds, and a mat made from coconut husks. During the summer, the uncovered edge moved back 5 metres. The covered area did not move. Culha says this is very promising. However, scientists say the result is from only one slump and one summer. It is hard to measure exactly how much CO2 the blankets prevent. Culha thinks one blanket could stop about 2,200 tonnes of CO2 a year. If they install 20,000 blankets, they might prevent 40 million tonnes a year. That is about the same as Hungary's yearly emissions. The company wants to sell carbon credits for the emissions they prevent. They have raised 1.4 million Canadian dollars from investors. They hope to issue credits as soon as next year.", "vocab": [{"word": "permafrost", "pos": "noun", "zh": "永久冻土", "note": "指常年冻结的土壤"}, {"word": "blanket", "pos": "noun", "zh": "毯子；覆盖层", "note": "这里指覆盖在冻土上的材料"}, {"word": "emissions", "pos": "noun", "zh": "排放物（如二氧化碳）", "note": "常指温室气体排放"}, {"word": "thaw", "pos": "verb", "zh": "融化；解冻", "note": "指冻土因升温而融化"}, {"word": "erosion", "pos": "noun", "zh": "侵蚀", "note": "指土壤或土地被水、风等破坏"}, {"word": "slump", "pos": "noun", "zh": "塌陷坑", "note": "文中指冻土融化形成的凹陷区域"}, {"word": "carbon dioxide", "pos": "noun", "zh": "二氧化碳", "note": "一种温室气体，化学式CO2"}, {"word": "carbon credits", "pos": "noun", "zh": "碳信用", "note": "一种可交易的减排凭证"}], "title_original": "Woody ‘blankets’ are being deployed on Arctic permafrost to reduce carbon emissions", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 08 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03045-8\nA start-up company is testing whether stabilizing areas of rapidly eroding permafrost can prevent greenhouse-gas emissions.", "body_original": "NEWS\n08 October 2026\nWoody ‘blankets’ are being deployed on Arctic permafrost to reduce carbon emissions\nA start-up company is testing whether stabilizing areas of rapidly eroding permafrost can prevent greenhouse-gas emissions.\nBy\nJames Dinneen\nJames Dinneen\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nA Canadian start-up firm is developing a way to prevent greenhouse-gas emissions from permafrost by stabilizing the soil with an insulating ‘blanket’ of woody materials. If the method works, it could be the first tool to directly forestall green-house gas emissions that are escaping from permafrost as the Arctic heats.\n“Our goal is to make sure the carbon in the soil remains buried,” says Cansu Culha, co-founder of PermaC and a natural-hazards researcher at the University of British Columbia in Vancouver, Canada.\nEarth’s\nfrozen environments are thawing\nas temperatures rise in response to human emissions. The only reliable way to slow that melting on a global scale is to slash emissions from the burning of fossil fuels. But some researchers are exploring methods to prevent the thaw directly, ranging from placing reflective mats on glaciers to pumping water onto sea ice to thicken it.\nResearchers say that PermaC’s approach is the first they know of to focus on preventing permafrost thaw and its\nassociated emissions\n, which are estimated to be equivalent to those of Japan.\nThe company just completed the first test of its insulating blankets in Canada’s Northwest Territories at an area of rapidly eroding permafrost known as a retrogressive thaw slump. These slumps occur when a hill of permafrost thaws and collapses, enabling erosion to take big bites out of the landscape. This exposes deeper layers of soil to warming, forming hotspots of CO\n2\nand methane emissions. One estimate suggests that these areas of rapid erosion could amplify permafrost emissions by as much as 40% compared to models that only account for gradual thaw.\nThe company’s approach is to stabilize the edge of the slumps with a layer of wood shavings, soil and native seeds, and a burlap-like mat of coconut husks. Culha says she got the idea after hearing about Indigenous communities in Alaska that prevent permafrost degradation by planting trees along the edges of slumps. Similar erosion-control blankets are used in temperate areas, but they haven’t been used to insulate or stabilize permafrost until now, she says.\nPermaC has yet to release results from this year'stest. But the blanket seems to have halted erosion: over the Arctic summer, the edge of the slump without the blanket retreated by 5 metres, whereas the blanketed area saw no measurable retreat, Culha says.\n“That’s very promising,” says Christina Schaedel, a permafrost scientist at the Woodwell Climate Research Center in Falmouth, Massachusetts, who has been advising the company. However, the result is from only one slump over one summer, she adds.\nGreen blanket\nOne challenge is calculating how much CO\n2\nthe blankets prevent from being emitted. Measuring the CO\n2\nemissions from slumps is not straightforward and remains an active research question, says Mark Lara, a biologist who studies permafrost landscapes at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign. “There definitely are slumps that are spewing out carbon,” he says. But there are “lots of minutiae that could dramatically impact the results”, he adds. For instance, the emissions from any given slump depend on its geometry, the amount of carbon in the soil and how much of that carbon ends up as CO\n2\nor methane after erosion.\nSo far, Culha has only rough estimates for the emissions that the blankets might prevent. Extrapolating from research on erosion rates and soil carbon in the Arctic, she thinks that about 2,200 tonnes of CO\n2\na year might be prevented from being emitted at the average carbon-rich slump. If those calculations are accurate and the blankets can be installed at 20,000 slumps across Canada and Alaska — the company’s ambitious goal over the next decade — she estimates that they might prevent about 40 million tonnes of CO\n2\nemissions a year, roughly equivalent to the annual emissions of Hungary.\nCulha says the company is developing a detailed methodology that a third party could use to verify prevented emissions, which would be necessary for the company to sell carbon credits for emissions the blankets prevent. People could purchase those credits to offset emissions elsewhere. The firm aims to issue credits as soon as next year, and has so far raised Can$1.4 million (US$1 million) from investors, says Culha.\nEnjoying our latest content?\nLog in or create an account to continue\nAccess the most recent journalism from Nature's award-winning team\nExplore the latest features & opinion covering groundbreaking research\nAccess through your institution\nor\nSign in or create an account\nContinue with Google\nContinue with ORCiD\ndoi: https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-03045-8\nReprints and permissions\nRelated Articles\nEpic blazes threaten Arctic permafrost. Can firefighters save it?\nThis Arctic town wants to make renewable energy work at the top of the world\nIs lightning striking the Arctic more than ever before?\nThe Arctic is burning like never before — and that’s bad news for climate change\nSubjects\nClimate change\nEngineering\nEnvironmental sciences\nLatest on:\nClimate change\nEngineering\nEnvironmental sciences\nAustralia’s bush fires got worse after British colonization — this is why\nNews\n07 OCT 26\nThe Great Barrier Reef requires special attention in the developing super El Niño\nCorrespondence\n06 OCT 26\nNature has rights — it is time the world recognized them\nComment\n02 OCT 26\nA 7-eV bandgap semiconductor based on silicon-doped α-(AlxGa1−x)2O3\nArticle\n07 OCT 26\nA quadruped robot designed to complete a marathon on a single battery charge\nArticle\n23 SEP 26\nAnodic Pd membrane H2 extraction enhances thermochemical dehydrogenation\nArticle\n09 SEP 26\nAustralia’s current wildfire crisis linked to colonial land-use change\nArticle\n07 OCT 26\nNature has rights — it is time the world recognized them\nComment\n02 OCT 26\nAgricultural liming is a carbon sink in the Mississippi River Basin\nArticle\n23 SEP 26\nJobs\nFaculty Positions at the Center for Machine Learning Research (CMLR), Peking University\nCMLR's goal is to advance machine learning-related research across a wide range of disciplines.\nBeijing (CN)\nCenter for Machine Learning Research (CMLR), Peking University\nAssistant or Associate Professor of Cell Biology\nDepartment of Cell Biology at the University of Virginia School of Medicine invites applications for Assistant or Associate Professor of Cell Biology\nCharlottesville, Virginia\nUniversity of Virginia\nRecruitment Announcement for High-Level Talent at the Center for Agricultural Microbiology\nJoin HZAU's global faculty team to advance research with competitive benefits.\nWuhan, Hubei (CN)\nHuazhong Agricultural University (HZAU)\nOpen-Rank Faculty Position in Theoretical Biology\nThe Department of Biological Sciences seeks an outstanding individual for an open-rank faculty position in Theoretical Biology.\nNashville, Tennessee\nVanderbilt University Department of Biological Sciences\nProteomics Specialist\nBuild the science that shapes the future of human health. Application closing date: 08 November 2026 Join a place where ambitious science thrives...\nMilan (IT)\nHuman Technopole", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T12:42:12.257593+00:00", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "A", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.25, "sm": 100, "em": 250, "cs": 0, "ce": 1, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "Canadian", "s": 0.263, "e": 0.825, "sm": 262, "em": 825, "cs": 2, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "start-up", "s": 0.838, "e": 1.312, "sm": 837, "em": 1312, "cs": 11, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "called", "s": 1.325, "e": 1.612, "sm": 1325, "em": 1612, "cs": 20, "ce": 26, "si": 0